#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091944 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092338 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e380013-0002-5a17-bbf7-e9ce89e6331d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Indicator Function of a Set"}] :question/id #uuid "2acf7f4e-1d5d-56e5-8478-9611a3afde73" :question/math-academy-id "e-12766" :question/problem "![](images/45/45a423930fb07c365f10cdfbdeb3d45ef337078fa77e1b0699388088438d4c70.png)\n\n \n\nWhat is the subset $A$ of $U=\\{ 1,2,3,4,5 \\}$ whose indicator function $\\boldsymbol{1}_A(x)$ is shown above?" :question/worked-solution "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n![](images/15/159dcc0eb98804f6d4d281603de45cf00922023ce1d427ff73a7188bea38e40d.svg)\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $1,2,$ and $5$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{ 1,2,5 \\}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091945 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092339 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd399539-33cf-5738-b970-fcc6c1d919c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using Properties of Indicator Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "cc45cc10-61e3-5cb7-babc-7253a5158c4b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12767" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{1}_{A}(-1) = 0, \\qquad \\mathbf{1}_{B}(-1) = 1\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 0$\n\nIII. $-1 \\in A \\cup B$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overline{A},$ $A \\cap B,$ and $A \\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(-1) & =max{{1}_{A}(-1),{1}_{B}(-1)} \\\\  & =max{0,1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1,$ we conclude that $-1$ belongs to $A \\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092338 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e380013-0002-5a17-bbf7-e9ce89e6331d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Indicator Function of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd26958-8f1d-57a3-abb9-807967b6f0e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a,b,d}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409846} {:answer/id #uuid "31e4f699-7440-595b-9f7d-fd1e34e533f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{c,e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409848} {:answer/id #uuid "a5edb5b5-0109-53a0-8948-f6dbe9ed80b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{d,e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409864} {:answer/id #uuid "b32466f0-2c65-5408-a0aa-16fe5815df3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a,b,c}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409867} {:answer/id #uuid "bb9403e9-592b-58af-9902-144607686e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a,b,c,d}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fd26958-8f1d-57a3-abb9-807967b6f0e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a,b,d}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409846} :answer-field/id #uuid "d41cebf3-62a2-5784-a1f4-13fc481b665c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3c4858f-5a41-55b3-bb89-5356156d99e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-172706" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b2919328788c6762d1a0b56225b95749e88973ec84803584be0d336f63033566.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the subset $A$ of $U={a,b,c,d,e}$ whose indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ is shown above?" :question/worked-solution "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)={\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $a,b,$ and $d$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A={a,b,d}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092339 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd399539-33cf-5738-b970-fcc6c1d919c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using Properties of Indicator Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1737c9b3-9afe-57ff-999b-4fa97a61723d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409844} {:answer/id #uuid "49cb469a-be52-5791-8807-8b802f15d5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409854} {:answer/id #uuid "844ca5b4-a0c6-5ba0-9623-57c490152861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409861} {:answer/id #uuid "95407129-ac58-5f82-82c8-97ce4f4156cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409863} {:answer/id #uuid "f0066faa-d1eb-5427-ab3c-9e44126bd655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1737c9b3-9afe-57ff-999b-4fa97a61723d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409844} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dc6e1da-5a6c-5d24-ae2f-76a4c9c09715" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dbfb19de-ef84-5bc1-a24a-a4632bbb8c2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-172721" :question/problem "$$\n{1}_{A}(5)=1,\\,{1}_{B}(5)=0\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5)=1$\n\nII. ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5)=0$\n\nIII. $5\\in A\\cap B$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5) & =min{{1}_{A}(5),{1}_{B}(5)} \\\\  & =min{1,0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5)=0,$ we conclude that $5$ does not belong to $A\\cap B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092338 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e380013-0002-5a17-bbf7-e9ce89e6331d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Indicator Function of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1325aba8-981f-532b-b892-12eb6305d32e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a,c,e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409841} {:answer/id #uuid "315c03d9-5543-5355-95b3-ced1d45e7088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{b,c,e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409847} {:answer/id #uuid "3df49fbd-f52c-571e-843c-9a57fe7a2605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{d,c,d}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409850} {:answer/id #uuid "6b58a9c1-6929-5a77-a9db-024a96962cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a,b,c}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409857} {:answer/id #uuid "6e77902f-8af5-5f32-b8fb-71bb215e7d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a,d}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "315c03d9-5543-5355-95b3-ced1d45e7088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{b,c,e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409847} :answer-field/id #uuid "72880716-881b-572f-a482-436f0d9e99ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a66f5e5d-805e-5011-9534-19a9e92d3193" :question/math-academy-i